<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah [Laws of the Afternoon Prayer]<br>Halachos 1–2</div>

<div class="sn">Halachah 1 — <i>Minchah</i> Is the Awakening from the Sleep of Commerce<br><span style="font-size:.9rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:35</span> (<i>Ashrai HaAm</i>)</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="h">Halachah</span>: A person should not sit before the barber close to <span class="h">Minchah</span> time, etc. The <span class="h">Shulchan Aruch</span> discusses the opinions that specifically close to <span class="h">Minchah</span> the Sages decreed [not to begin certain activities]; see there.</div>

<div class="p">It appears to my humble understanding that <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> is the aspect of the awakening from sleep. Throughout the day, a person is occupied with his affairs, his commerce [<span class="h">masa u'matan</span>]. Commerce is the aspect of sleep — therefore <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> was established to remind man to remember Hashem and awaken from his sleep (= from the commerce, which is the aspect of sleep). Therefore <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — the awakening from the sleep that is commerce. As Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> writes all this in the teaching "<span class="h">Ashrai HaAm</span>" (<span class="lr">LM I:35</span>); see there. Yitzchak established <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>, and <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> is <span class="h">Pachad Yitzchak</span> [the Fear of Yitzchak].</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the prayer is called by the name <span class="h">Minchah</span> — a <span class="h">Minchah</span> being the offering of the poor man, the aspect of <span class="h">dinim</span> [judgments], for then judgments prevail — the aspect of <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, the aspect of <span class="h">Pachad Yitzchak</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is what our Sages say: <span class="mx">"one should always be careful with <i>Tefilas HaMinchah</i>, for Eliyahu was answered only at <i>Minchah</i> time."</span> Eliyahu's request and prayer was to reveal <span class="h">emunah</span> in the world — to know that <span class="v">"Hashem, He is G-d"</span> (as explained there). He was answered only at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time — because <span class="h">Minchah</span> is beneficial for <span class="h">emunah</span>, based on what is explained: <span class="h">Minchah</span> is the awakening from sleep. Through this, <span class="h">emunah</span> is renewed, as Rabbainu writes: during commerce the soul enters into <span class="h">emunah</span> (= commerce in <span class="h">emunah</span>) and is renewed there. Afterward, when one awakens from sleep, the mind is renewed with additional <span class="h">emunah</span>. <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= the awakening from sleep) — through this one merits <span class="h">emunah</span>. Therefore Eliyahu merited then to reveal <span class="h">emunah</span> in the world: <span class="v">"Hashem, He is G-d."</span></div>

<div class="p">Since <span class="h">emunah</span> is in the aspect of <span class="v">"this is Yerushalayim — in the midst of the nations I have placed her"</span> and the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> [external forces] draw sustenance from her — one must be very careful. One needs caution and exertion not to remain submerged there, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> — not to let them overpower, since they draw sustenance from there. This is: <span class="mx">"one should always be careful with <i>Tefilas HaMinchah</i>"</span> — then extra caution is needed. Therefore the Sages decreed that specifically close to <span class="h">Minchah</span> one should not begin any work or eating — lest he forget to pray. Specifically then they were concerned, since then judgments prevail — the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> drawing from <span class="h">emunah</span>. Therefore they decreed these decrees — lest he forget, for then extra caution is needed lest they overpower him.</div>

<div class="sn">Halachah 2 — <i>Minchah</i>, Charity, and the Revelation of the Will<br><span style="font-size:.9rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM II:4</span> (<i>Uvyom HaBikurim</i> / Rav Yehudah HaNadvoah)</span></div>

<div class="p">Based on the teaching "<span class="h">Uvyom HaBikurim</span> — of Rav Yehudah HaNadvoah"; <span class="lr">LM II:4</span>. See the entire teaching there well.</div>

<div class="p">The summary that emerges: through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> one repairs the flaw of the elders of the generation who are not as they should be. Then the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> [forehead of the serpent] (= the root of natural wisdom, which draws its sustenance from there) is subdued. Then the voice of the call of <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> is heard — the revelation of the Will — through connecting to the root of Will, the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> [forehead of Will] (= the aspect of the passing of Moshe, etc.). The voice of the roaring of the evil creatures (= the wise men of nature, etc.) is subdued. See there well. Through the revelation of the Will, awe is achieved; through awe, <span class="h">chesed</span> flows — and then one need not engage in any work or commerce: the world is sustained through His <span class="h">chesed</span>, and <span class="v">"strangers shall stand and tend your flocks."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 1 — Prayer Is the Revelation of the Will; Evil Thoughts Come from Evil Creatures</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">tefilah</span>. <span class="h">Tefilah</span> is the revelation of the Will — through prayer to Hashem, it is revealed that everything is by His will. Natural wisdom is subdued, for <span class="h">tefilah</span> changes nature (nature dictates one thing, but <span class="h">tefilah</span> changes it — as explained in Rabbainu's words many times). <span class="h">Tefilah</span> is the revelation of the Will — the aspect of <span class="h">Yom Tov</span>. Therefore three daily <span class="h">tefilos</span> were established corresponding to the three <span class="h">Regalim</span> [Festivals] (which are the revelation of the Will), and also corresponding to the three Avos: Avraham established <span class="h">Shacharis</span>, Yitzchak established <span class="h">Minchah</span>, etc. The three <span class="h">tefilos</span> are in the aspect of the three <span class="h">Regalim</span> — the revelation of the Will. Therefore the essential <span class="h">tefilah</span> is when there is an <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> [a time of favor]: <span class="v">"as for me, my prayer is to You, Hashem — at a time of favor."</span> An <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> is the revelation of the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> — then <span class="h">tefilah</span> has power and is received.</div>

<div class="p">The essential disturbance of <span class="h">tefilah</span> and the foreign thoughts during prayer come from the evil creatures (opposed to the holy creatures: lion, ox, eagle = the three <span class="h">tefilos</span>). Against them are dog, donkey, hawk — evil creatures that snatch and tear, disrupting the three <span class="h">tefilos</span> with foreign thoughts. These evil creatures are the "wise men of nature" explained in the above teaching — who deny the Will (as explained there). The essential foreign thoughts in <span class="h">tefilah</span> come through the corruption of <span class="h">emunah</span> (as explained well in Rabbainu's words). Foreign thoughts come only from the evil creatures (= the wise men of nature who deny the Will, which is the opposite of <span class="h">tefilah</span>). <span class="h">Tefilah</span> is essentially through <span class="h">emunah</span> — believing everything is by His will. Foreign thoughts (= opposing <span class="h">tefilah</span>) come through the evil creatures (= wise men of nature who deny the Will = opposing <span class="h">emunah</span>).</div>

<div class="p">But when the root of the Will is revealed (the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span>), the evil creatures (= wise men of nature) are subdued — then it is an <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> and all <span class="h">tefilos</span> and requests are received, since the <span class="h">tefilos</span> are then proper, without any admixture of foreign thoughts. When <span class="h">tefilah</span> is pure, without foreign thoughts, it is certainly received and rises favorably before Him.</div>

<div class="p">Even the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s prayer, which is completely pure — sometimes his request is not fulfilled, as the holy Zohar states: <span class="mx">"sometimes He hears, sometimes He does not hear."</span> Nevertheless the <span class="h">tzadik</span>'s prayer certainly makes an impression and is certainly received favorably — only that Hashem does not fulfill the request. In truth, we do not understand Hashem's ways.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the essential <span class="h">tefilah</span> is to focus on the <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> — when the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> is revealed: the evil creatures (= wise men of nature) are subdued, foreign thoughts in <span class="h">tefilah</span> are subdued, the <span class="h">tefilah</span> is pure, clean, and complete — received favorably. This is what Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa said: <span class="mx">"if my prayer is fluent in my mouth, I know it is received; if not, I know it is <i>m'turaf</i> [torn apart]."</span> <span class="h">M'turaf</span> [torn] specifically — the aspect of the evil creatures that snatch and tear, overpowering against the <span class="h">tefilah</span>. Rashi explains: <span class="mx">"<i>m'turaf</i> — they tear his prayer in his face."</span> Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa understood from his prayer (which was certainly pure, without any admixture of foreign thoughts): when he felt the evil creatures overpowering — he knew this from his prayer not being fluent in his mouth, the words not flowing and being spoken in order (as Rashi explains). When there is some disturbance, the prayer is not fluent. He understood: when his prayer was not fluent — certainly the evil creatures were overpowering, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Even the great <span class="h">tzadik</span> has a war in his prayer, according to his level, in great subtlety. <span class="mx">"If my prayer is fluent in my mouth"</span> — with no delay or prevention in the prayer — this indicates the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> is revealed and the evil creatures opposing prayer are subdued — <span class="mx">"then I know it is received"</span> (when the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> is revealed, it is an <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> and the prayer is received). <span class="mx">"If not"</span> — indicating no <span class="h">ais ratzon</span>, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, and the evil creatures overpower — <span class="mx">"I know it is <i>m'turaf</i>"</span>: torn, specifically.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one must give <span class="h">tzedakah</span> before <span class="h">tefilah</span> — to subdue the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> (which opposes the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span>). Then the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> is revealed — it is an <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> and the prayer is received: <span class="v">"as for me, my prayer is to You, Hashem — at a time of favor."</span></div>

<div class="p">This is what our Sages say about the early pious ones [<span class="h">chassidim harishonim</span>]: <span class="mx">"they would wait one hour before praying, one hour in prayer, etc."</span> They asked: <span class="mx">"when was their work done?"</span> They answered: <span class="mx">"since they were pious, their Torah was preserved and their work was blessed."</span> Based on the above teaching: through being pious and devoting so much time to prayer (praying properly), their work was blessed of its own — they needed no commerce or labor. For <span class="h">tefilah</span> is the revelation of the Will; through the Will's revelation, awe is achieved; through awe, <span class="h">chesed</span> flows — then one needs no commerce: <span class="v">"strangers shall stand and tend your flocks."</span> These pious ones — through properly praying, their work was blessed of its own (needing no labor: <span class="v">"strangers shall stand"</span>). Therefore <span class="h">tefilah</span> is called <span class="h">yir'ah</span> [awe] — through <span class="h">tefilah</span> (= revelation of the Will), awe is achieved.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 2 — <i>Minchah</i> Time: When Judgments Are Strongest and Extra Vigilance Is Needed</div>

<div class="p">It emerges: man must focus in his <span class="h">tefilah</span> to arouse and reveal the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> (the root of the Will), so it will be an <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> and his prayer will be received. Through the revelation of the root of the Will, he can concentrate in prayer and overcome the evil creatures (= the wise men of nature who deny the Will, from which foreign thoughts come). One must be especially careful and exert himself in <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span> — for <span class="mx">"at <i>Minchah</i> time, harsh judgment prevails"</span>; from the extension of these judgments comes the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>'s hold. Therefore the evil creatures (= wise men of nature from the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>) overpower more, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, during the prevailing of judgments — at <span class="h">Minchah</span> — since their hold derives from there. These evil creatures (= wise men of nature who deny the Will) are in the aspect of <span class="h">rogez</span> [anger/agitation] — the opposite of Will: <span class="v">"short of days and full of <i>rogez</i>"</span> (as explained well in the above teaching). They are the aspect of <span class="h">rogez</span> — their hold coming from <span class="h">rogez</span> and judgments. Therefore they are called evil creatures that snatch and tear — through their intensified <span class="h">rogez</span>.</div>

<div class="p">At <span class="h">Minchah</span> (when harsh judgment prevails), greater exertion is needed against the evil creatures (= wise men of nature = <span class="h">rogez</span>). The essential sustenance of the evil creatures comes from the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> (= the root of natural wisdom). The <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> draws from the elders of the generation — those who grow old without adding holiness and <span class="h">da'as</span> each day. Man must add holiness and <span class="h">da'as</span> with each passing day. Whoever flaws his days — growing old without this addition — from such elders the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> draws sustenance (as explained there well).</div>

<div class="p">Upon reflection: the essential sustenance of the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> (drawing from the generation's elders) is specifically at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time — as the day turns, the day ascends above. Whoever added holiness and <span class="h">da'as</span> that day — fortunate is he. But when the flawed days of the aforementioned elders ascend — those who did not add holiness and <span class="h">da'as</span> that day, flawing it — from this the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> overpowers, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span> (= <span class="h">rogez</span>). The essential sustenance from flawed elders is at <span class="h">Minchah</span> — when their day has turned, departed in darkness and flaw without holiness. Perhaps this is why harsh judgment prevails at <span class="h">Minchah</span> — many flawed days of such elders ascend, and from this judgments intensify (the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> drawing from those flawed days = <span class="h">rogez</span>, the opposite of Will). At <span class="h">Minchah</span>: the prevailing of judgments (= <span class="h">rogez</span>). Therefore one must exert himself greatly in <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>, concentrating deeply — overcoming the evil creatures that overpower then.</div>

<div class="p">The essential thing: to see to arouse and reveal then specifically the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> (= the <span class="h">ais ratzon</span>). The essential revelation of the <span class="h">metzach haratzon</span> (= the <span class="h">ais ratzon</span>) is at <span class="h">Minchah</span> of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> — when Moshe's passing occurred (as brought). Therefore even on weekdays at <span class="h">Minchah</span>, one must draw the holiness of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> into the weekday — arousing and revealing at the weekday <span class="h">Minchah</span> specifically the <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> as on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>: to sweeten the harsh judgment and <span class="h">rogez</span> that awakens at <span class="h">Minchah</span>. <span class="mx">"From it itself the axe-handle comes to fell it"</span> — specifically at <span class="h">Minchah</span> when harsh judgment and <span class="h">rogez</span> are aroused, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, then specifically the judgment and <span class="h">rogez</span> are sweetened through the <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> drawn from <span class="h">Shabbos</span> into the weekdays. But for this, <span class="h">tzedakah</span> is needed — to subdue the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> (as explained well in the above teaching).</div>

<div class="p">Now the wonder of the <span class="h">poskim</span> [legal authorities] is well resolved — they wondered why this prayer is specifically called <span class="h">Minchah</span>, since the <span class="h">Minchah</span> offering was also brought in the morning (as the <span class="h">Magen Avraham</span> discusses in <span class="h">siman</span> 232). Based on Rabbainu's words in the above teaching, it is well understood: the essential tikun of this prayer is through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> — through which the flaw of the generation's elders is repaired, the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> (which draws sustenance from them) is subdued, and the Will is revealed. Therefore it is called <span class="h">Minchah</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">tzedakah</span>: <span class="v">"those who bring the <i>Minchah</i> in righteousness [<i>b'tzedakah</i>]."</span> As Rabbainu writes there: <span class="h">tzedakah</span> is especially needed at the time of this prayer.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 3 — Yaakov and Esav: The <i>Minchah</i> as <i>Tzedakah</i> to Sweeten Judgment</div>

<div class="p">This is what Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> brings there: Yaakov said to Esav: <span class="v">"take my <i>minchah</i> [gift] from my hand."</span> Esav is the contamination of the serpent — the aspect of the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span>. He opposes Yaakov, who is the aspect of Will — the aspect of the Face [<span class="h">panim</span>]: <span class="v">"seekers of Your face — Yaakov, <i>selah</i>."</span> <span class="h">Panim</span> is the aspect of Will: <span class="v">"Hashem shall lift His face to you"</span> (as Rabbainu writes in the above teaching). Esav's essential sustenance is from Yitzchak (= <span class="h">Tefilas HaMinchah</span>, for Yitzchak established <span class="h">Minchah</span>). The hold of the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> is from the aspect of <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= judgments). Its strengthening, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, occurs when the generation's elders are not as they should be — awakening the judgment of <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= Yitzchak). Though Yitzchak is holy judgment, when there is a flaw, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, the holy judgment is aroused and from it all other judgments take hold and receive.</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="v">"when Yitzchak grew old"</span> — when the judgment of Yitzchak is aroused through the aforementioned elders (= the arousal of <span class="h">Minchah</span>'s judgments): <span class="v">"he called Esav"</span> — the holy judgment is compelled, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, to give sustenance to Esav (= the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span>) from the flaw of the generation's elders. But Hashem in His mercy informs Yaakov (= the <span class="h">tzadik</span>, the aspect of Will), urging him to preemptively appease the judgment through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> — giving food to the poor, in whom the judgments are bound (as brought). Whoever gives them <span class="h">tzedakah</span> and feeds them — thereby appeases the judgment.</div>

<div class="p">The <span class="h">Shechinah</span> (= Rivkah) urges Yaakov to go to the flock (= the holy flock, Israel) and take from them two goat kids to make delicacies for Yitzchak (= the judgment) — to receive from them <span class="h">tzedakah</span> for the poor (who are "broken vessels" in which judgments are bound). Through the <span class="h">tzedakah</span> given to them, Yitzchak's judgment is appeased and the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> (which draws from the generation's elders) is subdued.</div>

<div class="p">Yitzchak was astonished when Yaakov said: <span class="v">"Hashem your G-d arranged it for me"</span> — a great wonder in Yitzchak's eyes. Esav (= the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span>, natural wisdom) wanted now to draw sustenance and overpower against the Will. Yet now he hears the very opposite: <span class="v">"Hashem your G-d arranged it"</span> — attributing all causes and events to Hashem. Therefore he said: certainly this is <span class="v">"the voice of Yaakov"</span> (= Will) — believing everything is solely by Hashem's will, with no natural cause, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. Since Yitzchak's judgment was sweetened and Yaakov (= Will) prevailed — he received the blessings: <span class="v">"may G-d give you of the dew of heaven"</span> — blessed that His great <span class="h">chesed</span> pour upon us until we need no commerce or labor: <span class="v">"nations shall serve you"</span> (= <span class="v">"strangers shall stand and tend your flocks"</span>). Through subduing the <span class="h">metzach hanachash</span> (= Esav) and the Will being revealed — <span class="h">chesed</span> flows: <span class="v">"may G-d give you of the dew of heaven... abundant grain and wine"</span> — abundant <span class="h">chesed</span> without our handiwork, only <span class="v">"nations shall serve you."</span> He mentioned <span class="h">Elokim</span> — <span class="v">"may <i>Elokim</i> give you"</span> — for <span class="h">chesed</span> must be received through awe (which comes through the Will, as explained there); <span class="h">Elokim</span> is the aspect of awe: <span class="v">"I fear G-d [<i>Elokim</i>]."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 4 — Yaakov Sends a <i>Minchah</i> of <i>Tzedakah</i> to Subdue Esav</div>

<div class="p">Therefore, afterward when Yaakov confronted Esav, he said: <span class="v">"take my <i>minchah</i> from my hand"</span> — hinting that he is giving the aspect of <span class="h">tzedakah</span>: <span class="v">"your oppressors shall be <i>tzedakah</i>"</span> — the aspect of <span class="h">Minchah</span> (as explained in the above teaching; see there). All of this was to repair and subdue Esav's sustenance, which he draws from the aspect of <span class="h">Minchah</span> (= Yitzchak). Therefore he gives the aspect of <span class="h">tzedakah</span> to repair this.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 5 — Eliyahu at <i>Minchah</i>: Subduing the Worship of Nature Through the Revelation of Will</div>

<div class="p">This is: <span class="mx">"one should always be careful with <i>Tefilas HaMinchah</i>, for Eliyahu was answered only at <i>Minchah</i>."</span> Eliyahu wanted then to subdue the idol-worshippers of his day. Their essential idolatry was the worship of stars and constellations — attributing everything to celestial arrangements, therefore worshipping them to draw their powers downward. Eliyahu needed to subdue natural wisdom (which attributes all causes to celestial arrangements) and reveal that <span class="v">"Hashem, He is G-d"</span> — all causes are by His will alone.</div>

<div class="p">Those who stood against Eliyahu strengthened themselves until <span class="h">Minchah</span> time — thinking they would have sustenance and power through <span class="h">Minchah</span>, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. But Eliyahu sweetened the judgment of <span class="h">Minchah</span> — therefore he prevailed over them specifically at <span class="h">Minchah</span>. When the judgment of <span class="h">Minchah</span> is sweetened, it becomes an <span class="h">ais ratzon</span>. <span class="v">"At the time of the <i>Minchah</i> offering, Eliyahu drew near"</span> — then specifically. He prayed and revealed the Will through the great sign and wonder he performed. They all answered: <span class="v">"Hashem, He is G-d; Hashem, He is G-d"</span> — everything is through Hashem alone, through His will. He rules, governs, and directs everything by His will. All of this Eliyahu did specifically at <span class="h">Minchah</span> — by being able to sweeten <span class="h">Minchah</span>'s judgment, transforming it into an <span class="h">ais ratzon</span> as on <span class="h">Shabbos</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This was accomplished through <span class="h">tzedakah</span> — the aspect of: <span class="v">"I have commanded the ravens to sustain you"</span> (as explained well there). Regarding Eliyahu this verse is said — close to the above event: after the episode when he was hidden and the ravens sustained him, he went to appear before Achav and then subdued the worshippers of the Ba'al. Therefore Eliyahu had the power to sweeten <span class="h">Minchah</span>'s judgment, transforming it into Will — through the aspect of <span class="v">"I have commanded the ravens to sustain you."</span> <span class="mx">"From it itself the axe-handle comes"</span> — since he merited transforming the cruelty of the raven into mercy (to sustain him), through this he also had the power to sweeten <span class="h">Minchah</span>'s judgment, transforming it into Will — until all acknowledged: <span class="v">"Hashem, He is G-d."</span></div>

<div class="cn">✦ End of Hilchos Tefilas HaMinchah, Halachos 1–2 ✦<br><i>Na Nach Nachma Nachman May'Uman</i></div>

